WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:07.480
FARRELL: Good morning. Thank you all for joining
us. I have the distinct privilege today of

00:00:07.480 --> 00:00:14.480
welcoming you to the Council on Foreign Relations,
and the honor of presenting the IMF's managing

00:00:15.440 --> 00:00:21.060
director, Madame Lagarde, on her first public
appearance of the year, I understand.

00:00:21.060 --> 00:00:26.000
Clearly she needs no introduction, but I do
want to highlight what I think is the very

00:00:26.000 --> 00:00:30.480
special place that she holds in world history.
And I mean that.

00:00:30.480 --> 00:00:35.170
She was the first female chair of Baker McKenzie
-- the law firm, the international law firm.

00:00:35.170 --> 00:00:40.870
She was the first finance minister of a G8
country, and of course now she's the first

00:00:40.870 --> 00:00:46.760
head of the IMF. But maybe more importantly
in the context of that, right in the worst

00:00:46.760 --> 00:00:52.809
part of 2009, worst crisis, she was ranked
by the FT as the best finance -- best minister

00:00:52.809 --> 00:00:58.050
of finance of the Eurozone. And of course
we all know here in DC she enjoys that kind

00:00:58.050 --> 00:01:02.180
of reputation in her new role here.
So it is my distinct pleasure and honor to

00:01:02.180 --> 00:01:07.000
introduce to you Madame Lagarde, who will
share with us what's next with the global

00:01:07.000 --> 00:01:11.610
economy. We will then turn to some questions
I have prepared for her, and leave plenty

00:01:11.610 --> 00:01:17.320
of time to get your questions. Thank you very
much.

00:01:17.320 --> 00:01:24.320
LAGARDE: Well, thank you very much, Diana,
and good morning to all of you, and happy

00:01:28.710 --> 00:01:33.150
new year to all of you because yes, it is
my first sort of public appearance, and I'm

00:01:33.150 --> 00:01:39.900
delighted to be here at the Council on Foreign
Relations, for the opportunity to speak today

00:01:39.900 --> 00:01:46.820
about the world economy, as we see it from
the perspective of the IMF.

00:01:46.820 --> 00:01:50.579
I think the Council -- I was checking�that
just before I came in. The Council and the

00:01:50.579 --> 00:01:57.579
IMF actually have something in common. They
were both created after a world war conflict,

00:01:58.290 --> 00:02:03.909
the Council after the First World War, and
the IMF after the Second World War. So our

00:02:03.909 --> 00:02:10.509
genesis has certainly something in common.
And so does our mission. You're clearly focusing

00:02:10.509 --> 00:02:17.290
on peace and stability, and our mission, as
embedded in the articles of the IMF that were

00:02:17.290 --> 00:02:24.290
signed in Bretton Woods in 1944, is precisely
stability and prosperity. So there's no better

00:02:25.319 --> 00:02:31.849
moment at the beginning of 2015 to talk about
prosperity, to talk about stability, and certainly

00:02:31.849 --> 00:02:38.849
to talk about peace.
I think it's particularly important in 2015

00:02:40.870 --> 00:02:47.870
because from a personal perspective, I can
assure you that teamwork, team spirit and

00:02:48.010 --> 00:02:55.010
a collective approach will be not only expected
but required.

00:02:55.430 --> 00:03:01.349
I wasn't marching the streets of Paris on
Sunday, I was marching the streets of Washington.

00:03:01.349 --> 00:03:08.019
And it was incredibly comforting to see the
number of not just French expats but also

00:03:08.019 --> 00:03:14.849
many American friends who were marching down
the streets of Washington in support, with

00:03:14.849 --> 00:03:21.849
that collective spirit about us that unites
many of us around key values. So that collective

00:03:23.200 --> 00:03:30.060
approach and that team spirit was also clearly
demonstrated with the line of leaders and

00:03:30.060 --> 00:03:37.060
all those who participate in the same pursuit
of the same objectives on this occasion.

00:03:39.459 --> 00:03:46.459
Now, many of you will expect numbers, and
many of you will be disappointed, because

00:03:48.299 --> 00:03:55.299
we are publishing our World Economic Outlook
update in a week's time, and out of respect

00:03:55.750 --> 00:03:59.980
for the team and for the research department
in particular, I'm not going to mention the

00:03:59.980 --> 00:04:06.980
numbers. I will leave that to them.
But I can already say this. Despite the boost

00:04:10.120 --> 00:04:17.120
from oil prices and despite a stronger U.S.
growth, we see the global recovery continuing

00:04:19.060 --> 00:04:26.060
to face a very strong headwind, and that's
what I'm going to discuss with you this morning.

00:04:27.070 --> 00:04:34.070
But I would like to first of all kick off
with a little story. Two months ago, on November

00:04:36.639 --> 00:04:43.639
12, 2014, a little spacecraft operated by
the European Space Agency made the first ever

00:04:46.020 --> 00:04:53.020
landing on a comet after a ten-year journey
covering half a billion miles. And like millions

00:04:59.030 --> 00:05:06.030
of other people, not necessarily into astronomy
and the study of the cosmos, I was just gobsmacked

00:05:08.430 --> 00:05:15.430
by what they did.
They touchdown on the comet called 67P was

00:05:17.880 --> 00:05:24.880
part of the ongoing Rosetta mission, which
is led by a multinational team of scientists.

00:05:25.810 --> 00:05:31.979
And together, they have extended humanity's
reach into the cosmos, and by doing so, they

00:05:31.979 --> 00:05:38.979
have enhanced our understanding of our own
planet. Rosetta -- that will ring a bell with

00:05:40.090 --> 00:05:47.090
some of you. Rosetta embodies the spirit of
the 19th century scientists, who in their

00:05:48.300 --> 00:05:55.270
own time used actually the Rosetta stone to
decode the ancient Egyptian hieroglyphs.

00:05:55.270 --> 00:06:01.830
Now, why is this story relevant today, other
than to demonstrate that Europe can actually

00:06:01.830 --> 00:06:06.449
do really impressive things?
(LAUGHTER)

00:06:06.449 --> 00:06:12.780
Well, because I think that this year, the
global economy will face what we might call

00:06:12.780 --> 00:06:19.780
three Rosetta moments 
if they want to accomplish the international
mission that I will talk about. Those major

00:06:28.639 --> 00:06:34.759
policy challenges -- think about meteorites
that are coming our way. They will require

00:06:34.759 --> 00:06:41.580
decisions based on political courage, decisive
action and multilateral thinking, cooperation,

00:06:41.580 --> 00:06:48.580
a bit like those scientists demonstrated.
So the first Rosetta moment is about boosting

00:06:51.880 --> 00:06:58.880
growth and creating employment. The second
Rosetta moment is about achieving not any

00:06:59.069 --> 00:07:06.069
growth but more inclusive and better shared
growth. And the third Rosetta moment is about

00:07:07.340 --> 00:07:14.340
making sure that that growth is sustainable
and balanced.

00:07:14.620 --> 00:07:21.620
Now, the three of them are clearly deeply
interconnected. All are important and all

00:07:23.819 --> 00:07:30.819
demand strong leadership. They all require
cooperation. But you know what? If we can

00:07:32.169 --> 00:07:39.169
catch a comet out in space with a�ten years'
lag and half a billion miles in between, sure

00:07:40.160 --> 00:07:46.800
we can address the policy challenges that
were facing right here on planet earth.

00:07:46.800 --> 00:07:52.960
So I will start with the first Rosetta, the
immediate challenge of how to inject greater

00:07:52.960 --> 00:07:59.960
momentum into the recovery. As I said, I'm
not releasing numbers, but I would like to

00:08:00.190 --> 00:08:07.190
give you at least an understanding of trends
and policy recommendations. And the obvious

00:08:08.550 --> 00:08:15.550
questions that we have at the moment is, should
lower oil prices and a stronger recovery in

00:08:16.940 --> 00:08:23.940
the United States make us more upbeat about
the prospect for the global economy? And unfortunately,

00:08:25.210 --> 00:08:32.210
most likely, the answer is no.
No. No why? Because there are powerful factors,

00:08:35.490 --> 00:08:42.490
legacies of the crisis, that are still with
us and that are weakening our economies. Clearly,

00:08:46.670 --> 00:08:53.550
the drop in oil price is, as we say, a shot,
and a welcome shot in the arm for the global

00:08:53.550 --> 00:08:59.130
economy. But to pursue the physical analogy,
shot in the arm is good, but if the global

00:08:59.130 --> 00:09:05.380
economy is weak on its knees, it's not going
to help. We need both strong arms, strong

00:09:05.380 --> 00:09:12.380
legs, as well.
Cheaper oil increases consumer purchasing,

00:09:13.450 --> 00:09:20.000
increases private demand in oil-importing
countries. And depending on how long oil prices

00:09:20.000 --> 00:09:27.000
remain low, this could provide a positive
contribution to global growth for some times.

00:09:28.160 --> 00:09:34.760
As for the U.S. economy, it performed well
in 2014, and in our view, it should strengthen

00:09:34.760 --> 00:09:41.760
even more in 2015, largely due to more robust
household spending.

00:09:43.800 --> 00:09:48.200
I'm not paying too much attention to the latest
number that was published this morning. To

00:09:48.200 --> 00:09:55.200
be checked, but it doesn't change our perspective.
U.S. unemployment continues to decline. Cheaper

00:09:56.709 --> 00:10:02.209
oil is boosting real incomes and consumer
sentiment, and there is continued support

00:10:02.209 --> 00:10:08.950
for accommodative monetary policy.
So what is the catch? The oil price and the

00:10:08.950 --> 00:10:15.910
U.S. growth are not a cure to deep-seated
weaknesses elsewhere. Too many countries are

00:10:15.910 --> 00:10:22.910
weighed down by at least two factors -- legacies
of the financial crisis -- high debt, high

00:10:22.950 --> 00:10:29.790
unemployment. Too many companies and households
keep cutting back on investment and consumption

00:10:29.790 --> 00:10:32.970
today because they are concerned about the
growth tomorrow.

00:10:32.970 --> 00:10:39.970
In fact, if you look at the overall map, the
United States is probably the only major economy

00:10:41.589 --> 00:10:48.589
that is likely to buck the trend this year,
while others are being held back. There are

00:10:53.850 --> 00:10:58.230
some promising recoveries in some places,
and there is certainly promising recovery

00:10:58.230 --> 00:11:05.230
taking hold and continuing in the United Kingdom,
for instance, while growth remains very low

00:11:05.880 --> 00:11:12.300
in the Euro area and Japan. And emerging economies
that were, remember, the drivers for growth

00:11:12.300 --> 00:11:17.110
a couple of years ago, are slowing down -- in
relative terms, granted, but they are slowing

00:11:17.110 --> 00:11:22.050
down, as is China, for instance.
So that's the overall situation as we see

00:11:22.050 --> 00:11:27.279
it, where the low price of oil, while probably
a net positive at the end of the day, depending

00:11:27.279 --> 00:11:33.790
on the content of supply and demand driving
it, and a strong U.S. economy, will not suffice

00:11:33.790 --> 00:11:40.560
to actually accelerate the growth or the potential
for growth in the rest of the world.

00:11:40.560 --> 00:11:46.470
So I mentioned the strong headwinds. What
do I mean by that? And what risks are we facing

00:11:46.470 --> 00:11:53.470
-- you know, those meteorites coming at us?
First risk, the asynchronous normalization

00:11:56.190 --> 00:12:01.920
of monetary policies in advanced economies.
And for those of you -- many, I'm sure -- who

00:12:01.920 --> 00:12:08.589
have studied ancient Greek, you will remember
that the "a" before anything means exactly

00:12:08.589 --> 00:12:14.579
the opposite of what the word means. So asynchronicity
of monetary policy simply means that there

00:12:14.579 --> 00:12:21.579
is no synchronicity. And for the former synchronized
swimming that I was, asynchronicity is to

00:12:21.760 --> 00:12:25.860
be watched.
(LAUGHTER)

00:12:25.860 --> 00:12:32.019
And what I mean by asynchronicity is super-asynchronicity
because we can legitimately expect that the

00:12:32.019 --> 00:12:39.019
Fed will change monetary policy further, as
announced, as very well communicated so far,

00:12:41.350 --> 00:12:48.350
to move into interest rates variation, while
at the same time -- so exit from unconventional

00:12:49.700 --> 00:12:53.950
monetary policy on the part of the Fed, while
at the same time very likely, as well -- although

00:12:53.950 --> 00:13:00.639
not yet, granted, other central banks -- including
the ECB, will move into more unconventional

00:13:00.639 --> 00:13:04.550
monetary policy.
So you have the combination of an exit and

00:13:04.550 --> 00:13:11.550
one -- one major entry and continuation by,
for instance, the Bank of Japan. There's been

00:13:13.550 --> 00:13:20.550
a lot of talk about it, and what we can certainly
expect from the talented chairmanship of the

00:13:22.620 --> 00:13:29.490
Fed is continuous good communication, and
that will be very important going forward.

00:13:29.490 --> 00:13:36.490
But we should certainly expect, as well, volatility
of capital flows as a result of this asynchronous

00:13:36.800 --> 00:13:43.800
monetary policies around the world. And who
says volatility of capital flows is also likely

00:13:44.529 --> 00:13:51.529
to expect potential negative effects for emerging
markets and global financial stability.

00:13:51.800 --> 00:13:58.800
Now, amongst those emerging markets, some
are likely to face triple risks. First of

00:14:01.269 --> 00:14:06.899
all, the strengthening of the U.S. dollar.
I'm talking emerging markets here -- strengthening

00:14:06.899 --> 00:14:13.440
of the U.S. dollar. Second, higher global
interest rates. And third, more volatile capital

00:14:13.440 --> 00:14:17.899
flows.
And that will have a significant impact on

00:14:17.899 --> 00:14:24.690
financial systems in emerging markets because
many banks, many large corporates have actually

00:14:24.690 --> 00:14:31.690
borrowed in U.S. dollars, and they've increased
their borrowing over the last five years.

00:14:31.760 --> 00:14:37.959
The oil price drop, and weaker commodity prices
more generally, have added to these risks,

00:14:37.959 --> 00:14:44.959
with some countries such as Nigeria, Russia,
Venezuela facing huge currency pressures.

00:14:46.350 --> 00:14:50.480
And given -- and there are other countries,
but these ones are clearly the three major

00:14:50.480 --> 00:14:57.100
ones. And given their size, the recent developments
in those countries could have ramification

00:14:57.100 --> 00:15:03.459
effects on a regional basis, what we call
the spillover consequences.

00:15:03.459 --> 00:15:10.459
Third area of risk is that the Euro are, and
Japan could remain stuck in a world that I

00:15:13.149 --> 00:15:20.149
would call low-low -- low growth, low inflation.
And that would make it even harder for many

00:15:21.740 --> 00:15:28.740
Euro area countries to reduce very high unemployment
and excessive public and private debt and

00:15:31.360 --> 00:15:38.360
would, as a result, raise the potential risk
of recession and deflation.

00:15:39.540 --> 00:15:45.540
Fourth risk is one that we hear about, watch
on television on a regular basis and those

00:15:45.540 --> 00:15:52.540
that we call the geopolitical risks. I'll
take an example -- Ukraine, which we are doing

00:15:52.800 --> 00:15:59.800
a lot of work on. Increased international
support to complement the IMF support is crucial.

00:16:03.850 --> 00:16:10.850
At the same time, while you have those spots
of geopolitical tensions, which can be identified,

00:16:18.100 --> 00:16:24.779
with which you can talk -- we can talk, there
is also this palpable sense that the forces

00:16:24.779 --> 00:16:30.300
of intolerance and fragmentation are gaining
strength and sometimes competing with each

00:16:30.300 --> 00:16:37.300
other. The recent atrocities in France, my
home country, but also in Nigeria or in Pakistan,

00:16:40.149 --> 00:16:47.060
are only the latest actions of forces that
are fundamentally opposed to what we here

00:16:47.060 --> 00:16:54.060
in this room believe in.
Now, you take all these four risks of different

00:16:54.139 --> 00:17:01.139
natures applied to the current economic situation,
and I would say that this calls for a powerful

00:17:02.300 --> 00:17:07.260
policy mix that can strengthen the recovery
and provide better employment perspectives

00:17:07.260 --> 00:17:14.260
for the citizens of the world. That's the
first Rosetta moment, better recovery, more

00:17:16.289 --> 00:17:22.500
employment.
Now, how can the policy makers deliver? To

00:17:22.500 --> 00:17:28.590
show solidarity, toexpress common interest
is good, but there's a bit more to be done,

00:17:28.590 --> 00:17:32.980
as well. I'll look at the various policies
available.

00:17:32.980 --> 00:17:39.309
First of all, accommodative monetary policies
remain essential, given the numbers that we

00:17:39.309 --> 00:17:46.309
have. Second, fiscal adjustment must be as
growth and as employment-friendly as is possible.

00:17:49.220 --> 00:17:55.010
And above all, the policy makers have to step
up structural reforms. There's a lot of talk

00:17:55.010 --> 00:18:02.010
about it. Some are doing it. But there has
to be real implementation.

00:18:02.520 --> 00:18:09.520
All of that is not new, but it takes a sense
of increased urgency and it requires increased

00:18:11.390 --> 00:18:18.390
political leadership. For example -- and I
will take an example that is on our daily

00:18:19.559 --> 00:18:26.559
radar screen, the price of oil. It will have
an impact, but it will be a test for many

00:18:28.780 --> 00:18:35.780
policy makers -- not so much for the oil importers,
for whom the windfall provides an opportunity

00:18:36.490 --> 00:18:42.580
to strengthen their macroeconomic framework
and may help in alleviating inflation pressure.

00:18:42.580 --> 00:18:49.580
But oil exporters need to cushion the shock
on their economies. Now, some are using their

00:18:51.990 --> 00:18:58.990
rainy day funds that they were wise enough
to set up in the first place. And countries

00:18:59.820 --> 00:19:03.750
like Saudi Arabia, for instance, and a few
other in the Middle East, are going to actually

00:19:03.750 --> 00:19:10.750
for the first time in many, many years have
actually fiscal deficits. Others will resort

00:19:12.289 --> 00:19:19.289
to allowing substantial exchange rate depreciation,
which will certainly raise inflation and require

00:19:24.340 --> 00:19:31.340
tighter monetary policies.
In the Euro area, cheaper oil should be a

00:19:31.950 --> 00:19:38.360
positive, right? Most of them are importers,
and not all of them have 80 percent of their

00:19:38.360 --> 00:19:45.360
needs supplied by nuclear power energy. But
it could also contribute to a further decline

00:19:47.780 --> 00:19:53.039
in inflation expectations, which increases
the risk of deflation, that you will remember,

00:19:53.039 --> 00:19:58.280
I have mentioned in respect of both the Euro
area and Japan.

00:19:58.280 --> 00:20:05.280
This bolsters the case for additional monetary
stimulus, which I'm very pleased to see the

00:20:06.200 --> 00:20:13.200
European Central Bank is considering. More
on that January 22nd, or thereafter.

00:20:13.850 --> 00:20:19.059
Now, most importantly -- and we at the IMF
are very pleased to have seen this development

00:20:19.059 --> 00:20:26.059
lately -- the drop in oil prices provides
a golden opportunity for those countries that

00:20:26.539 --> 00:20:33.539
have subsidized the use of fossil fuel energy
to actually reduce their subsidies. You know,

00:20:34.919 --> 00:20:41.919
the price of oil declines, they can take out
portion or all of the subsidies that were

00:20:42.570 --> 00:20:47.710
given to all consumers, in many instances,
to consume at decent prices.

00:20:47.710 --> 00:20:52.270
Well, prices are becoming decent by virtue
of the supply and demand operations at the

00:20:52.270 --> 00:20:57.640
moment. And we were very pleased to see that
many countries have actually embarked on that

00:20:57.640 --> 00:21:02.499
project that we've been advocating. And the
list is -- well, I'll mention a few countries

00:21:02.499 --> 00:21:09.499
-- Jordan, Tunisia, Cameroon, Cote d'Ivoire,
Egypt, Haiti, India, Indonesia, Malaysia -- all

00:21:09.890 --> 00:21:16.630
these countries have embarked on that journey.
And I think that bodes very well for my third

00:21:16.630 --> 00:21:23.630
Rosetta that I will mention in a little while.
Does that concern only those countries that

00:21:24.240 --> 00:21:31.240
have subsidized energy? No. It could also
include some advanced economies for which

00:21:32.150 --> 00:21:38.220
it might be time to consider seriously what
is sometimes called the gas tax or the carbon

00:21:38.220 --> 00:21:45.220
tax -- more sophisticated but same approach.
In order to do what? To build fiscal buffers,

00:21:47.059 --> 00:21:53.679
yes. To finance needed infrastructure projects,
maintenance of roads.

00:21:53.679 --> 00:21:59.320
For instance, I happen to cycle in Washington,
D.C....

00:21:59.320 --> 00:22:04.100
(LAUGHTER)
-- or to reduce other taxes, such as taxes

00:22:04.100 --> 00:22:09.400
on labor in those countries where unemployment
is high. It doesn't have to be an increase

00:22:09.400 --> 00:22:16.400
of tax. It can perfectly be tax-neutral, but
better thought through, with a better basis.

00:22:18.230 --> 00:22:25.230
But it will require political courage.
Now my second Rosetta. We've looked at the

00:22:32.419 --> 00:22:38.340
situation as it is, we've looked at the risks,
and we've identified policies. My second Rosetta

00:22:38.340 --> 00:22:43.450
is about a more inclusive and better shared
growth.

00:22:43.450 --> 00:22:50.450
Let me be blunt. For more than six years after
the start of the great recession, too many

00:22:51.430 --> 00:22:56.929
-- and despite growth, except one year -- I'm
talking global numbers here -- many people

00:22:56.929 --> 00:23:03.929
do not feel that we are out of the crisis.
And in too many countries, unemployment remains

00:23:04.370 --> 00:23:11.370
high and inequality has increased.
This is why we need a decided push for structural

00:23:11.890 --> 00:23:18.890
reforms to boost current and potential growth.
2015 must therefore be a year of action. It

00:23:21.120 --> 00:23:28.120
means removing deep-seated distortions in
labor and product markets. It means revamping

00:23:29.320 --> 00:23:36.320
creaking infrastructures and building new
ones. It means trade liberalization and pressing

00:23:37.260 --> 00:23:42.330
ahead with reforms on education, health and
social safety nets.

00:23:42.330 --> 00:23:49.330
It also means unleashing the economic power
of an underdeveloped segment of our societies

00:23:50.919 --> 00:23:56.529
when it comes to�creation of�value. And
I'm here talking about the women. Let me touch

00:23:56.529 --> 00:24:03.529
on two areas that I've just mentioned --�infrastructure,
women.

00:24:07.390 --> 00:24:12.779
Infrastructure investment -- am I talking
about the bridge to nowhere? No. We highly

00:24:12.779 --> 00:24:18.210
value infrastructure and we highly encourage
it as a policy going forward, provided that

00:24:18.210 --> 00:24:25.210
it is efficient, that it is carefully chosen,
and that it actually does two things -- it

00:24:25.840 --> 00:24:32.840
increases the output in the short term, whether
it's publicly induced or totally publicly

00:24:33.539 --> 00:24:39.200
funded, or partly public and private. The
investment in infrastructure actually contribute

00:24:39.200 --> 00:24:45.020
in boosting the output for the short term,
boosting demand.

00:24:45.020 --> 00:24:51.110
And in the long term -- and that's where you
have the double whammy as a result of investing

00:24:51.110 --> 00:24:58.110
in infrastructure -- in the long term, it
improves the productivity and the productive

00:24:58.120 --> 00:25:04.279
capacity of the countries or the regions or
the cities where infrastructure is being financed

00:25:04.279 --> 00:25:11.230
and developed. And this is no surprise that
the G20 has actually identified major infrastructure

00:25:11.230 --> 00:25:16.529
investment as one of its lead projects going
forward.

00:25:16.529 --> 00:25:22.130
The whole growth agenda, by the way, of the
G20 over the next four years, and the Australian

00:25:22.130 --> 00:25:29.130
leadership last year, is actually forecasted
to increase GDP by $2 trillion U.S., if they

00:25:32.059 --> 00:25:37.770
all do what they committed to do.
Now, the scope of such investment is going

00:25:37.770 --> 00:25:44.669
to vary from country to country. If you sit
in India or in Brazil, you're going to want

00:25:44.669 --> 00:25:51.669
to invest in removing bottlenecks -- bottlenecks
-- and focusing probably on transportation

00:25:52.070 --> 00:25:59.070
and coordinated transportation and energy.
If you look at the U.S. and Germany, well,

00:26:00.419 --> 00:26:07.419
it's not going to be the same approach, but
it's certainly going to involve financing

00:26:08.419 --> 00:26:15.419
the maintenance of existing infrastructure
and possibly looking at creating new infrastructure.

00:26:15.840 --> 00:26:22.669
Why is that? Because if you look at numbers,
for decades, in both those countries, the

00:26:22.669 --> 00:26:29.490
United States and Germany, there has been
very little spent on the maintenance of infrastructure

00:26:29.490 --> 00:26:35.179
and the development of new ones.
In the same vein, certainly, the plan articulated

00:26:35.179 --> 00:26:41.799
by Mr. Juncker, the new president of the European
Commission, of setting up this fund of $315

00:26:41.799 --> 00:26:47.900
billion is also to be encouraged, particularly
if it attracts private money together with

00:26:47.900 --> 00:26:54.900
it. So much for infrastructure.
Gender policies -- you know, that's a major

00:26:57.340 --> 00:27:04.340
potential game changer. There are millions
of women who are locked out of the labor market,

00:27:06.230 --> 00:27:12.390
locked out of the economy as we measure it.
And excluding these women is not just morally

00:27:12.390 --> 00:27:19.390
wrong, but it's an economic mistake.
Gender gaps in labor participations are everywhere

00:27:23.820 --> 00:27:30.820
in the world. Now, they vary from an average
of about 12 percent in the OECD countries

00:27:31.200 --> 00:27:37.649
to about 30 percent in the Middle East and
North Africa. And things can be done about

00:27:37.649 --> 00:27:42.409
it. If you look at the cases of Chile, for
instance, or the case of Sweden or the case

00:27:42.409 --> 00:27:48.830
of the Netherlands, using smart policies that
manage to actually reduce that gap and give

00:27:48.830 --> 00:27:55.830
better access to women, poor women, to the
labor market, whether it's investment in child

00:27:55.919 --> 00:28:02.919
care programs, whether it's putting in place
the legal framework that encourages without

00:28:04.159 --> 00:28:11.159
disincentivizing financially part-time systems,
these policies actually demonstrate that they

00:28:13.230 --> 00:28:17.740
work.
And that is another goal of the G20. I find

00:28:17.740 --> 00:28:23.200
it a little bit unambitious personally, but
at least it's on the map now, and the goal

00:28:23.200 --> 00:28:30.200
is to close the gender gap by 25 percent -- you
could have dreamed more, but 25 percent by

00:28:33.100 --> 00:28:40.100
2025. You could have expected less, but be
it as it may, if that was to happen, it will

00:28:40.510 --> 00:28:46.460
bring to the labor market 100 million more
women. And that means, obviously, a boost

00:28:46.460 --> 00:28:51.350
to the economy, global growth, and the reduction
of inequality.

00:28:51.350 --> 00:28:57.230
OK, so much for structural reforms, but how
do we leverage them? Where is growth going

00:28:57.230 --> 00:29:04.230
to come from? How will these infrastructures
be used? Where will these women be employed?

00:29:05.640 --> 00:29:12.059
There is at least one area that we are not
leveraging accordingly, and that is the area

00:29:12.059 --> 00:29:19.059
of trade and trade liberalization, because
that might very well provide the right answer.

00:29:21.080 --> 00:29:28.080
If you look at the number of global trade,
they go like that. It declines over time.

00:29:29.070 --> 00:29:36.070
And it's really about time that those numbers
be moving in the right direction, and there

00:29:36.679 --> 00:29:40.929
are ways to do that and those ways that are
just around the corner and available.

00:29:40.929 --> 00:29:47.929
One is certainly one that you know, the Trans
Pacific Agreement, TPA. The other one is the

00:29:51.070 --> 00:29:58.070
TTIP, which is the Transatlantic agreement,
which is probably the future category of trade

00:29:59.010 --> 00:30:05.059
agreement, more based on compliance, on standards,
on compatibility, rather than reduction of

00:30:05.059 --> 00:30:12.059
tariffs because reductions of tariffs between
those two zones have already been done. And

00:30:12.460 --> 00:30:18.230
in the United States, these important trade
deals are areas of potential cooperation between

00:30:18.230 --> 00:30:25.230
the new Congress and the president. So there
is hope, we hope.

00:30:25.450 --> 00:30:30.750
For the European Union, progress on trade
would be immensely helpful in lifting growth

00:30:30.750 --> 00:30:37.750
and confidence. The Japanese government is
very keen to reach closure and to use TPP

00:30:38.200 --> 00:30:45.200
in order to inject growth as an addition to
the three arrows of his Abenomics. And emerging

00:30:46.020 --> 00:30:50.340
and developing economies would benefit from
better integration into the global economy.

00:30:50.340 --> 00:30:57.340
So what's holding it? Well, on all sides,
there are incentives to cut deals. Political

00:30:59.700 --> 00:31:06.700
will, a collective approach, will be necessary
to get to the finish line.

00:31:07.110 --> 00:31:14.110
And I come to my third and last Rosetta moment.
How to achieve, in order to -- achieving growth,

00:31:14.690 --> 00:31:19.240
creating employment, doing it on a more inclusive
and better shared basis, including across

00:31:19.240 --> 00:31:26.240
gender -- how do we make sure that it is sustainable,
that it is more balanced?

00:31:29.260 --> 00:31:36.260
Three proposals here that we will be working
on or supporting this year -- financial regulation,

00:31:36.750 --> 00:31:43.750
international development and environmental
policies. The three of them are key.

00:31:44.049 --> 00:31:51.049
Now the first one is again becoming controversial.
Why should there be so much regulation? Why

00:31:53.090 --> 00:31:56.799
don't we have less? Why don't we have just
general principles, and we'll do the soft

00:31:56.799 --> 00:32:03.799
side amongst ourselves? I think if there is
one lesson that we learned from the great

00:32:03.929 --> 00:32:10.490
recession, it is that you cannot have sustainable
economic growth without having sustainable

00:32:10.490 --> 00:32:17.390
finance and a sustainable financial sector.
So we must complete the agenda on the financial

00:32:17.390 --> 00:32:24.390
sector reform. There has been progress, especially
on banking regulation, and to a lesser extent

00:32:24.600 --> 00:32:31.600
on addressing what I think is a key component
of that regulation, which is the "too important

00:32:31.820 --> 00:32:38.190
to fail" framework.
The global banking system is certainly less

00:32:38.190 --> 00:32:43.750
leveraged than it was, and in some countries,
it has shrunk by half. You know, if you take

00:32:43.750 --> 00:32:50.750
a country like Ireland, for instance, its
banking sector altogether has reduced, in

00:32:50.779 --> 00:32:57.779
terms of assets, by half.
Should there be joy about it,will ask some?

00:33:01.419 --> 00:33:07.380
It's just a fact that if you diagnosis -- if
you diagnose what has caused the collapse

00:33:07.380 --> 00:33:14.350
of Ireland and required that beautiful country
to actually go through a difficult program

00:33:14.350 --> 00:33:19.500
with success and courage, it's because the
banking system was largely inflated relative

00:33:19.500 --> 00:33:26.500
to the capabilities and the size of that economy.
Not the only cause, but certainly one.

00:33:28.289 --> 00:33:33.140
But if that has happened and if progress has
been made, there are more vulnerabilities

00:33:33.140 --> 00:33:40.140
coming out of other places that we call, conveniently,
the shadow banking system. Shadow banking

00:33:41.470 --> 00:33:47.600
is not bad in and of itself. It's fine if
it can be -- if it can be a resilient source

00:33:47.600 --> 00:33:54.600
of finance for the economy. But it's not good
if it's a nest of unpredictable hazards. And

00:33:56.429 --> 00:34:02.200
the size of the shadow banking sector has
in some places, or in some segment of their

00:34:02.200 --> 00:34:09.200
business, exceeded that of the banking sector.
So the big challenges facing the financial

00:34:09.679 --> 00:34:16.679
sector is now to implement the regulations
and to strengthen supervision. I'll give you

00:34:18.270 --> 00:34:23.659
a couple of examples where there has to be
more coordination, a more collective approach.

00:34:23.659 --> 00:34:30.659
On the implementation and interpretation
of the Basel 3 principles that said capital

00:34:30.889 --> 00:34:37.889
ratios, liquidity ratios, for instance, there
is a divergent interpretation and a divergence

00:34:38.099 --> 00:34:44.579
of views between two very critical markets,
the United States market and the European

00:34:44.579 --> 00:34:50.270
area market.
Second example of where policy makers have

00:34:50.270 --> 00:34:55.720
to reform further and make more progress -- on
the OTC derivative markets, where we are still

00:34:55.720 --> 00:35:02.540
very much in the unknown.
But what is, in my view, even more important,

00:35:02.540 --> 00:35:09.540
other than focusing on those areas where there
is a shortage of either regulations or supervision,

00:35:10.250 --> 00:35:17.250
or progress in getting there, is the ethical
dimension of that sector. And you know, we

00:35:19.310 --> 00:35:25.970
will hear more this year about the behavior,
the attitude and the ethical framework within

00:35:25.970 --> 00:35:31.579
which people operate because we will have
this year the first jury trials related to

00:35:31.579 --> 00:35:37.470
the LIBOR trading scandal.
And I really hope that this will be an occasion

00:35:37.470 --> 00:35:44.040
for the profession itself, for the financial
sector itself, to reflect on its mission,

00:35:44.040 --> 00:35:50.150
the ethical principles by which it operates,
and that they will not only see it as a list

00:35:50.150 --> 00:35:56.420
of rules for which compliance is needed under
the supervision of an army of lawyers and

00:35:56.420 --> 00:36:02.880
supervisors, but I hope there will be a reflection
on what's ethical, what's not ethical.

00:36:02.880 --> 00:36:09.880
Even if it's in compliance with the boxes
and they're all checked, that can actually

00:36:10.000 --> 00:36:17.000
do something when someone is not watching.
Certainly, as a mother, that's how I see the

00:36:18.570 --> 00:36:25.570
difference between what's ethical and what's
not. I'm not sure I've convinced my kids always.

00:36:27.660 --> 00:36:33.220
But certainly, restoring trust is going to
be an all-out effort not just on the part

00:36:33.220 --> 00:36:37.940
of policy makers, not just on the part of
regulators or supervisors, but it has to include

00:36:37.940 --> 00:36:44.940
the profession itself. They have to be part
and parcel of it.

00:36:44.990 --> 00:36:51.990
Another example of how to engender more sustainable
growth is through international development.

00:36:53.089 --> 00:36:57.940
Later this year, the United Nations will,
I hope, complete a major effort which has

00:36:57.940 --> 00:37:04.940
consisted in thinking through what will replace
the millennium development goals, many of

00:37:05.119 --> 00:37:10.060
which have been attained, with a new set of
goals that will be called the sustainable

00:37:10.060 --> 00:37:14.810
development goals.
We play an important role in that respect.

00:37:14.810 --> 00:37:21.810
We are helping with thinking through the financing
that will be needed to reach those goals.

00:37:23.390 --> 00:37:28.070
We will also, of course, make available our
expertise of working and dealing on a day-to-day

00:37:28.070 --> 00:37:31.960
basis with developing countries and with low-income
countries.

00:37:31.960 --> 00:37:38.960
2015 will also be a make-or-break year for
the effort to strike an international agreement

00:37:41.220 --> 00:37:48.030
on climate change. We hear about COP, that
will be it. It stands for the climate change

00:37:48.030 --> 00:37:55.030
conference where people will try to agree
on how -- by how much they will reduce the

00:37:58.940 --> 00:38:05.940
emission of various things, actually, not
only methane.

00:38:08.320 --> 00:38:15.320
To be convinced, all you need to do is look
at temperatures. They are rising. And 2014

00:38:15.720 --> 00:38:22.720
is the highest year in terms of temperature
ever since recording of temperature has begun.

00:38:23.170 --> 00:38:30.170
And it is also a year when the highest number
of natural catastrophe has taken place, natural

00:38:30.820 --> 00:38:37.820
disasters, more food and water scarcity�that
generally fuel conflicts, disputes, wars.

00:38:40.520 --> 00:38:47.070
Again, we need greater political courage to
reach that comprehensive deal to cut carbon

00:38:47.070 --> 00:38:54.070
emissions at the Paris summit in December.
And that brings me back to the beginning,

00:38:54.410 --> 00:39:00.550
and it's my conclusion. While I was speaking
-- and thank you very much, you were listening

00:39:00.550 --> 00:39:07.550
-- during that time, the Rosetta spacecraft
has continued to circle the dark, icy object

00:39:09.270 --> 00:39:16.270
that is comet 67P. And as the comet proceeds
closer to the sun, we may yet learn more of

00:39:18.930 --> 00:39:25.930
its secrets.
Look at the mission that put together that

00:39:29.210 --> 00:39:35.960
unbelievable success. They were diverse. They
came from different horizons. They didn't

00:39:35.960 --> 00:39:42.380
grow up together. And I think diversity has
had something to do with the success of that

00:39:42.380 --> 00:39:48.530
Rosetta mission, and it's certainly a true
testament to global cooperation. And policy

00:39:48.530 --> 00:39:54.589
makers should be inspired by that.
You know, there are no real secrets, or we

00:39:54.589 --> 00:40:01.589
have found the Rosetta long time ago to actually
identify what is needed for growth, what can

00:40:02.140 --> 00:40:08.520
engender it. But to deliver on the three Rosetta
moments, as I call them, the policy makers

00:40:08.520 --> 00:40:13.480
need to enhance global cooperation. They need
to embrace what I've called separately otherwise

00:40:13.480 --> 00:40:18.500
a new multilateralism.
And it should be put into practice whether

00:40:18.500 --> 00:40:25.099
it applies to COP, whether it applies to TPP,
whether it applies to TTIP, whether it applies

00:40:25.099 --> 00:40:32.099
to SDG -- you with me? This new multilateralism
also requires institutions that are efficient,

00:40:35.790 --> 00:40:41.430
that are credible, and are representative
of a changing global economy.

00:40:41.430 --> 00:40:48.430
And this is why, back in 2010, the international
community decided that the IMF should be reformed,

00:40:50.730 --> 00:40:56.290
that it should better represent the global
community, that it should be endowed with

00:40:56.290 --> 00:41:02.660
more capital -- it's called "quota" in our
institution -- and that some underrepresented

00:41:02.660 --> 00:41:07.190
countries, including some of the big emerging
market economies, should have a bigger voice

00:41:07.190 --> 00:41:14.190
around the table.
The IMF membership has called on the United

00:41:14.810 --> 00:41:21.810
States to ratify the 2010 reforms by the end
of last year, 2014. This was a commitment

00:41:23.369 --> 00:41:30.369
made to the G20 at the highest level. But
it hasn't happened.

00:41:31.619 --> 00:41:38.619
And I've spoken much about leadership today,
and I cannot but express some profound disappointment

00:41:38.790 --> 00:41:45.790
in the political powers who have so far failed
to grasp the benefits of reform, both for

00:41:47.000 --> 00:41:51.849
their own benefit, because maintaining stability
around the world, making sure that there are

00:41:51.849 --> 00:41:57.380
firefighters when something goes really wrong
in any of those geopolitical areas that I've

00:41:57.380 --> 00:42:04.380
mentioned, but also for the world at large.
We have seen so much fabulous leadership out

00:42:05.150 --> 00:42:12.150
of this country. I hope, I really hope, that
it is going to happen.

00:42:13.630 --> 00:42:20.630
Absent that, we are now working on interim
solutions to address some of the concerns

00:42:21.220 --> 00:42:28.220
that the other 187 countries have because
they have ratified. They are ready. But it

00:42:28.660 --> 00:42:34.690
can't go unless the country that has veto
power on all major decisions actually goes

00:42:34.690 --> 00:42:40.880
along.
But given the challenges that 2015 and the

00:42:40.880 --> 00:42:45.720
following years will bring, I don't think
that there is alternative to completing that

00:42:45.720 --> 00:42:51.319
2010 reform. We will be trying to find interim
solutions. We will be trying to address some

00:42:51.319 --> 00:42:58.319
of the issues. But the real thing is the 2010
reform that was actually very much engineered

00:42:59.180 --> 00:43:06.180
and advocated by the United States of America.
So I will continue to call on Congress to

00:43:06.310 --> 00:43:13.310
approve that reform without delay. And I have
trust in this country, so I know it will happen.

00:43:15.190 --> 00:43:21.359
Now, I would like to end with a quote that
encapsulates my call for greater leadership

00:43:21.359 --> 00:43:28.359
and cooperation in service of the global public
good. And that's a quote from -- and I had

00:43:28.650 --> 00:43:33.560
to move away from Egypt, you see? We've talked
a lot about Rosetta, so I'm turning to the

00:43:33.560 --> 00:43:40.250
Greek, to the ancient Greek, turning to Pericles.
Pericles -- you say? Pericles.

00:43:40.250 --> 00:43:47.170
He is known to have said -- and he knew something
about building. He knew something about architecture,

00:43:47.170 --> 00:43:53.930
no question about it. He said, What you leave
behind is not what is engraved in stone monuments

00:43:53.930 --> 00:44:00.640
but what is woven into the lives of others.
Well, I think we have a lot of weaving to

00:44:00.640 --> 00:44:07.640
do this year. Thank you.
(APPLAUSE)

00:44:08.540 --> 00:44:15.540
FARRELL: Thank you so much for that, a lot
of food for thought. But I want to go back

00:44:17.099 --> 00:44:22.490
to some of the themes that you started with,
if I may. You talked about the meteorites

00:44:22.490 --> 00:44:28.119
hitting the global economy. You talked about
strong headwinds, some of the deep-seated

00:44:28.119 --> 00:44:33.849
weaknesses, particularly Euro and Japan, and
some of the down sides.

00:44:33.849 --> 00:44:40.740
But you didn't quite go as far as stating
the risk of secular stagnation, which, as

00:44:40.740 --> 00:44:45.560
you know, is a common theme. We were both
at the Bretton Woods committee meetings where

00:44:45.560 --> 00:44:50.540
some were saying this is the seminal issue
of the day. Of course, by that, we mean the

00:44:50.540 --> 00:44:54.829
fact that the lack of demand drives a lack
of supply, which creates a vicious circle

00:44:54.829 --> 00:44:59.810
that reinforces itself over time.
Is that just one step too far, in your point

00:44:59.810 --> 00:45:05.520
of view? Do not believe the secular stagnation
story, or are there real risks when you put

00:45:05.520 --> 00:45:12.520
all -- your story together?
LAGARDE: You know, what I -- what we see is

00:45:13.089 --> 00:45:20.089
so much diversity, heterogeneity and fragmentation
both at the economic levels, at financial

00:45:22.160 --> 00:45:28.140
levels, at banking levels, that I hate to
-- shouldn't say that. I don't like to use

00:45:28.140 --> 00:45:34.410
one single concept, which is, you know, nice
to have, easy to mediatize, puts your name

00:45:34.410 --> 00:45:39.930
on headlines right away, because I think it
oversimplifies the matter.

00:45:39.930 --> 00:45:46.930
And you know, there are different ways to
look at the world, but we at the IMF are trying

00:45:47.069 --> 00:45:53.069
to drill down in each and every areas, and
each and -- you know, in each category. At

00:45:53.069 --> 00:45:58.900
the moment, clearly, the rise in the price
of oil is knocking down so many certainties

00:45:58.900 --> 00:46:03.530
and creating new categories in many ways.
You know, you have the oil importers. You

00:46:03.530 --> 00:46:08.660
have the oil exporters. You have those that
suffer the effect of the dollar because of

00:46:08.660 --> 00:46:12.619
where they are and whether they're pegged
or they're not pegged, whether they're hedged

00:46:12.619 --> 00:46:16.530
or not hedged. So there is a multiplicity
of current situation.

00:46:16.530 --> 00:46:22.500
You know, I think if you look at the Euro
area and Japan, we have a clear risk of what

00:46:22.500 --> 00:46:28.329
I call the low-low. I'm using different terminology,
as well. I just don't want to be bound by

00:46:28.329 --> 00:46:31.599
what has been said by many men before me,
so...

00:46:31.599 --> 00:46:35.869
(LAUGHTER)
FARRELL: So too blanket a statement, you would

00:46:35.869 --> 00:46:37.940
say, and more value in the granularity...
(CROSSTALK)

00:46:37.940 --> 00:46:42.940
LAGARDE: ... too broad for what we are seeing
as more and more and more diversity, in a

00:46:42.940 --> 00:46:47.150
world that continues to be highly -- it's
quite fascinating because you have the two

00:46:47.150 --> 00:46:52.530
movements. You have the vast interconnections
that continue, and you have a more and more

00:46:52.530 --> 00:46:59.180
diverse situation locally, and you have political
and geopolitical tensions that are operating

00:46:59.180 --> 00:47:04.619
in -- also very, very different way, with
a temptation by some or in -- you know, or

00:47:04.619 --> 00:47:10.109
by some countries, to actually, you know,
retreat and retire to your own turf and mind

00:47:10.109 --> 00:47:14.170
your own business, while at the same time,
there is an absolute need for a much more

00:47:14.170 --> 00:47:17.980
collective approach.
FARRELL: So let's turn to one area where maybe

00:47:17.980 --> 00:47:24.170
there is more sort of commonality, and that
is sort of low prices, low inflation, and

00:47:24.170 --> 00:47:29.619
in some cases, potentially deflation. Most
of the concern people had with the aggressive

00:47:29.619 --> 00:47:35.280
actions the Fed took, that the ECB may take,
had to do with the fear of inflation. It seems

00:47:35.280 --> 00:47:42.280
a far off fear, at the moment.
How well are economies equipped to deal with

00:47:42.420 --> 00:47:47.510
low inflation, maybe deflation? Is the IMF
itself equipped? Is that a real risk, in your

00:47:47.510 --> 00:47:51.260
mind, since most countries are facing it,
and the oil price, which most people see as

00:47:51.260 --> 00:47:55.950
a good thing, exacerbates that to some degree?
LAGARDE: Well, yes, it exacerbates it when

00:47:55.950 --> 00:48:00.809
you look at nominal inflation. When you look
at core inflation and expectations of core

00:48:00.809 --> 00:48:06.230
inflation, it doesn't have such an impact,
but it does at the nominal level.

00:48:06.230 --> 00:48:12.900
I think it's important to understand, you
know, why, despite the monetary policies we

00:48:12.900 --> 00:48:19.579
are seeing here or we might be seeing elsewhere,
there is nonetheless either flat, you know,

00:48:19.579 --> 00:48:25.530
or a very low inflation relative to the targets
that were set. And I think it has a lot to

00:48:25.530 --> 00:48:29.230
do with the output gap.
There are still in those countries -- maybe

00:48:29.230 --> 00:48:34.550
less so, and that's the reason why we might
be seeing shortly a change in monetary policy.

00:48:34.550 --> 00:48:40.710
But there are still available capacity. There
are still people who are looking for jobs

00:48:40.710 --> 00:48:46.069
and who have, you know, the ability to join
the market. So because of that, I think we're

00:48:46.069 --> 00:48:53.069
not yet seeing an uptick of inflation. And
clearly, in nominal terms, the oil prices

00:48:53.290 --> 00:48:57.569
is still keeping that down.
But you know, I'm not a central banker, but

00:48:57.569 --> 00:49:04.569
if I was, I would look very, very carefully
at core inflation and at inflation expectations

00:49:05.270 --> 00:49:11.410
in -- you know, in the two situations of exit
or entry into unconventional monetary policy.

00:49:11.410 --> 00:49:15.270
FARRELL: You raised so many issues. I literally
have ten questions I could ask you, but

00:49:15.270 --> 00:49:20.050
I'm not going to be selfish and I'm going
to turn for our last ten or so minutes to

00:49:20.050 --> 00:49:24.290
the audience and ask you for any questions
you may have for Madame Lagarde.

00:49:24.290 --> 00:49:27.839
UNKNOWN: (OFF-MIKE)
FARRELL: Please introduce yourself, your affiliation

00:49:27.839 --> 00:49:32.160
and share your question.
UNKNOWN: Paula Stern. I have my own consulting

00:49:32.160 --> 00:49:39.160
group, the Stern Group. And brava for a wonderful
presentation. My question goes back to oil

00:49:40.440 --> 00:49:47.059
and the dropping prices and the role of oil
in our whole traded sector. It is the most

00:49:47.059 --> 00:49:53.559
traded commodity, but it is not subject to
any of the WTO rules in the true sense of

00:49:53.559 --> 00:50:00.559
the word.
Are we at a place now where oil, traded oil,

00:50:00.579 --> 00:50:07.579
should be more subject to the rules of the
road when it comes to our trade policies?

00:50:10.170 --> 00:50:17.170
LAGARDE: You're raising a really interesting
question, and the -- the sleepy competition

00:50:19.390 --> 00:50:23.839
lawyer inside myself is waking up as you raise
that issue --

00:50:23.839 --> 00:50:27.890
(LAUGHTER)
-- because, you know, while there are independent

00:50:27.890 --> 00:50:34.099
producers, it's also -- I mean, there are
a couple of other commodities that operate

00:50:34.099 --> 00:50:41.099
along those lines. But it's -- it's a cartel.
And under many legislations around the world,

00:50:45.050 --> 00:50:52.050
cartels should not exist. So I'm not -- you
know, I'm not giving you exactly the answer,

00:50:55.069 --> 00:50:59.740
but I completely take your point, and I think
that there is -- there's a lot to be explored,

00:50:59.740 --> 00:51:04.859
actually. You know, there are other cartels
which have over the course of the last few

00:51:04.859 --> 00:51:11.500
years disappeared because of market forces.
So that might be an interesting one...

00:51:11.500 --> 00:51:14.829
(CROSSTALK)
FARRELL: ... have a question up there.

00:51:14.829 --> 00:51:21.099
UNKNOWN: Madame Lagarde, it's always a pleasure
to hear you speak. Alyse Nelson, CEO of�Vital

00:51:21.099 --> 00:51:25.520
Voices. First off, I want to thank you for
always being such a strong advocate and voice

00:51:25.520 --> 00:51:29.420
for women's empowerment. And I really enjoyed
your comments.

00:51:29.420 --> 00:51:32.349
I have a question for you because I think
it's something that we're really struggling

00:51:32.349 --> 00:51:37.410
with. I think it's totally indisputable, in
my opinion, that women were critical in helping

00:51:37.410 --> 00:51:41.809
to achieve not one but all eight of the post
-- of the millennium development goals. But

00:51:41.809 --> 00:51:46.430
I think when it comes to the post-2015 agenda,
and you know, the twenty-year anniversary

00:51:46.430 --> 00:51:51.190
of Beijing, we're still struggling with not
the awareness that women are quite critical,

00:51:51.190 --> 00:51:54.140
but really financing gender equality, which
you touched upon.

00:51:54.140 --> 00:51:58.800
And I wonder if you could talk a little bit
more about how we encourage, you know, multilateral

00:51:58.800 --> 00:52:02.809
institutions, governments to take this more
seriously because it is a strategy, it's not

00:52:02.809 --> 00:52:09.809
just a nice thing to do, as you said.
LAGARDE: I agree with you that the awareness

00:52:10.329 --> 00:52:17.329
has largely been achieved. I'm not sure that
the economic opportunities have been identified

00:52:21.150 --> 00:52:27.530
sufficiently on a very precise basis because
at the end of the day, you know, there is

00:52:27.530 --> 00:52:32.619
so much that you can say about the moral imperative,
but some people will turn their nose away

00:52:32.619 --> 00:52:36.640
and will say, you know, I'm only interested
by economic results, the bottom line, or whatever.

00:52:36.640 --> 00:52:42.569
So that's where we need to continue to document
and demonstrate that it makes imminent economic

00:52:42.569 --> 00:52:48.609
sense.
I have to -- I'll share something with you.

00:52:48.609 --> 00:52:53.740
We started doing some very discreet work about
the force and the role of women in the Japanese

00:52:53.740 --> 00:53:00.740
economy. And you know, it's an economy that
combines two things, three things. One, it's

00:53:02.130 --> 00:53:07.520
not particularly women-friendly in terms of,
you know, access to the job market. It's not

00:53:07.520 --> 00:53:14.520
specially immigration-prone. And it's aging.
I mean, something's got to give, right? And

00:53:16.869 --> 00:53:22.089
we documented, and I actually presented on
Japanese television for over an hour with

00:53:22.089 --> 00:53:29.089
other Japanese academics the value of women
and how they can change the Japanese economy

00:53:29.800 --> 00:53:34.309
over time because you can stimulate by putting
money as much as you want. If you don't have

00:53:34.309 --> 00:53:40.910
people to actually do the work, it's not going
to make any difference.

00:53:40.910 --> 00:53:47.910
And Prime Minister Abe has completely endorsed
that project, and he and his wife actually

00:53:49.839 --> 00:53:56.490
are really taking the leadership and championing
the cause. He has tasked his finance minister

00:53:56.490 --> 00:54:03.040
to actually identify enough budget to create
child care centers, and many more of them.

00:54:03.040 --> 00:54:08.690
I hope that they're now going to also soften
the regulations on the private child care

00:54:08.690 --> 00:54:15.690
facilities, which is extremely rigid in Japan.
And you know, those are legal things that

00:54:17.050 --> 00:54:21.710
matter, and financial support that matters,
as well.

00:54:21.710 --> 00:54:27.839
We will soon be publishing a study -- we're
doing that in February -- about the legal

00:54:27.839 --> 00:54:34.839
issues that constitute obstacles to the implementation
of better access, better economic contributions

00:54:38.849 --> 00:54:44.040
by women. It's amazing, actually. I mean,
it goes into all sorts of ramifications, from

00:54:44.040 --> 00:54:51.040
the tax, the design of income tax, to the
inheritance law, to the ability to put a name

00:54:54.750 --> 00:55:00.809
on a property title. I mean, there are lots
and lots of elements that contribute to that.

00:55:00.809 --> 00:55:07.450
But I'm a firm believer that you need a combination
of a good policy framework, specific labor

00:55:07.450 --> 00:55:13.780
measures intended to facilitate access, and
financing because child care centers are not

00:55:13.780 --> 00:55:16.280
going to just mushroom like that. They will
need to be funded.

00:55:16.280 --> 00:55:21.650
FARRELL: Let's take another question. Way
back there. Thank you. Yes, actually -- well,

00:55:21.650 --> 00:55:27.660
go ahead.
UNKNOWN: Thank you. Happy new year, Madame

00:55:27.660 --> 00:55:33.140
Lagarde. I'm Katerina Sokou with the Greek
daily Kathimerini. The crisis seems to be

00:55:33.140 --> 00:55:39.680
coming back to haunt Greece, and to what extent
does it affect the Eurozone at this moment,

00:55:39.680 --> 00:55:46.680
do you think? And to go back to your point
about leadership, how do you think Europe

00:55:48.440 --> 00:55:55.440
and the IMF -- and the IMF program -- relates
to what happens next in Greece? Thank you.

00:55:58.690 --> 00:56:05.640
LAGARDE: Well, first of all, we are all in
the waiting of the elections that are due

00:56:05.640 --> 00:56:11.549
on January the 25th. And as soon as that happens
and there is stability in the -- in whatever

00:56:11.549 --> 00:56:16.970
government is formed as a result of those
elections, we will pursue the discussions

00:56:16.970 --> 00:56:23.700
that we've had with the Greek authorities
in order to help the Greek economy to create

00:56:23.700 --> 00:56:30.700
growth, to have sustainable, and you know,
inclusive growth.

00:56:31.099 --> 00:56:38.099
The relationship with Europe I think has been
certainly better structured as a result of

00:56:38.109 --> 00:56:42.329
what the Europeans have changed about the
framework. I mean, the fact that there is

00:56:42.329 --> 00:56:47.480
an ESM, the European Stability Mechanism,
in place in order to avoid the systemicity

00:56:47.480 --> 00:56:53.119
and the contagion effect that could result
from a major crisis in any country, I think,

00:56:53.119 --> 00:56:58.359
is one important factor.
The current work that is being done about

00:56:58.359 --> 00:57:05.359
the interpretation of the stability and growth
pact in order to allow countries to pursue

00:57:05.480 --> 00:57:11.799
fiscal adjustment, but at a pace that is reasonable,
considering the economic circumstances, is

00:57:11.799 --> 00:57:16.890
also helping in that direction.
But you know, bottom line, Greece, like any

00:57:16.890 --> 00:57:23.890
other European country, is going to have to
go through structural reforms that are needed

00:57:24.970 --> 00:57:31.970
in order to sustain growth, in order to create
the conditions for investment and for employment

00:57:33.210 --> 00:57:39.010
creation. You know, it's common destiny, if
I may say.

00:57:39.010 --> 00:57:46.010
FARRELL: We'll now take you, sir.
UNKNOWN: Father Andrew from St. Paul's. Madame

00:57:49.510 --> 00:57:56.510
chairman, I'll give you another Greek quote
because we pray in ancient Greek. All good

00:57:58.630 --> 00:58:02.980
things flow into the city because of the city's
greatness. And when Solon said it, he was

00:58:02.980 --> 00:58:07.799
referring to the spiritual nature of Athens.
He wasn't referring to the temples and the

00:58:07.799 --> 00:58:14.799
gold. And it was also -- referenced Sparta
because of the amazing utilitarian pottery

00:58:15.589 --> 00:58:21.369
they had, which was not decorated, because
again, the city had great soul.

00:58:21.369 --> 00:58:26.809
Whether it's Greece or whether it's Portugal
or whether it's Italy, I'd like to know how

00:58:26.809 --> 00:58:32.079
you personally deal with the human cost. I
mean, I've held the hands of widows of people

00:58:32.079 --> 00:58:35.809
who -- you know, old pensioners who've committed
suicide because all of a sudden, their savings

00:58:35.809 --> 00:58:40.790
vanished overnight.
How much does the IMF really care about these

00:58:40.790 --> 00:58:47.790
things?
LAGARDE: Well, a lot. A lot, I can assure

00:58:47.829 --> 00:58:54.829
you. And you have to bear in mind one thing.
The IMF is asked to come to a country when

00:58:58.349 --> 00:59:03.119
a country has created the circumstances where
it's no longer handling its financial situation,

00:59:03.119 --> 00:59:08.250
where it has not taken the policy measures
that should have been taken in order to maintain

00:59:08.250 --> 00:59:12.890
stability in its economy.
So when I say that, I'm not trying to point

00:59:12.890 --> 00:59:17.569
the fingers at those policymakers over the
course of time, but I'm just trying to say

00:59:17.569 --> 00:59:24.569
that one cannot associate the difficult efforts,
the huge sacrifices sometimes, that have been

00:59:26.150 --> 00:59:32.819
made by people with the institution.
I wish I never had to go to Greece, Portugal.

00:59:32.819 --> 00:59:39.619
We don't have a program with Italy, but Iceland,
Ireland, Cyprus. Because if we didn't have

00:59:39.619 --> 00:59:46.400
to go there, it would have meant that the
policies in place, decided by policy makers,

00:59:46.400 --> 00:59:53.210
were good and maintained stability in those
economies. So that's point number one.

00:59:53.210 --> 01:00:00.210
Point number two -- when we help design the
various reforms which we believe are necessary

01:00:00.339 --> 01:00:07.339
for the country to restore its position, we
are always mindful of the poor people, of

01:00:07.540 --> 01:00:12.430
the safety nets that are needed.
I'll give you a very practical example. The

01:00:12.430 --> 01:00:19.430
pension system in Greece -- the average pension
in Greece is the same as the average pension

01:00:19.720 --> 01:00:25.359
in Germany. If you compare the GDP, not quite
the same. You compare the cost of living,

01:00:25.359 --> 01:00:31.790
not quite the same. But when we suggested
the reform of the pension system, we said,

01:00:31.790 --> 01:00:36.660
below certain thresholds, you don't -- you
cannot move the needle, you cannot change,

01:00:36.660 --> 01:00:41.670
you cannot put people in a situation where
it's just too hard.

01:00:41.670 --> 01:00:47.680
So we have that in mind, and we have in many
countries actually reinforced that point by

01:00:47.680 --> 01:00:54.680
saying, "safety net is a need in your reform".
We are, by the way, doing it also in all the

01:00:55.869 --> 01:01:00.270
reforms of the subsidy system because if you
take a country like Egypt, for instance, that

01:01:00.270 --> 01:01:07.270
is going through a reform of its subsidies
to fossil fuel energy, they cannot remove

01:01:07.309 --> 01:01:12.809
the subsidies just like that because there
are lots of poor people who rely on fuel to

01:01:12.809 --> 01:01:18.859
actually go to work or to do a little bit
to get by. Those people -- they cannot suffer

01:01:18.859 --> 01:01:24.030
as a result of the subsidy reform.
So they need to use public spending not in

01:01:24.030 --> 01:01:31.030
a uniform subsidization of energy, but they
use -- they have to use it to target those

01:01:31.680 --> 01:01:36.549
that actually need the money, by doing cash
transfers, for instance, which is what we

01:01:36.549 --> 01:01:40.510
often recommend.
FARRELL: I'm looking at the time very sadly

01:01:40.510 --> 01:01:45.920
and seeing we're just a couple of minutes
over. So maybe I'll just wrap up with a final

01:01:45.920 --> 01:01:52.920
question. This has been a busy twelve, eighteen
months of authorship on the themes that are

01:01:52.930 --> 01:01:58.020
so important to the IMF, if we think of the
Piketty book, if we think of the Martin Wolf

01:01:58.020 --> 01:02:01.400
book on what lessons we've learned from the
crisis and yet have to learn, if we think

01:02:01.400 --> 01:02:06.599
of -- I can -- (inaudible) recent book, you'll
know them all as well as I will. Is there

01:02:06.599 --> 01:02:08.299
one that you...
LAGARDE: Now, maybe you have time to read

01:02:08.299 --> 01:02:11.349
them. I don't read them all. I can't!
(LAUGHTER)

01:02:11.349 --> 01:02:15.450
FARRELL: Fair enough. OK. Is there one that
you think is particularly important for people

01:02:15.450 --> 01:02:19.520
who care about these issues that you think
has been a particularly seminal book of (inaudible)?

01:02:19.520 --> 01:02:23.630
LAGARDE: You know, I'm back to your first
question. Is there one definition for the

01:02:23.630 --> 01:02:27.829
entire world? I think to be well informed,
you have to read all these books, or at least,

01:02:27.829 --> 01:02:32.940
you know, a good, solid review of all of them,
and then pick and choose what your natural

01:02:32.940 --> 01:02:38.079
inclination takes you to, and then the other
ones because you need to have the dissenting

01:02:38.079 --> 01:02:44.869
views. You need to have different perspectives.
Otherwise, you are sort of -- you become narrow-minded.

01:02:44.869 --> 01:02:47.440
That's my sense. I wish I had time to read
them all.

01:02:47.440 --> 01:02:48.980
(LAUGHTER)
LAGARDE: I will one day.

01:02:48.980 --> 01:02:52.490
FARRELL: Some one will brief you, I'm sure.
Wonderful, thank you again very much for your

01:02:52.490 --> 01:02:52.569
thoughts. Thank you all for your questions.
(APPLAUSE)

01:02:52.569 --> 01:02:52.980
There is a lunch outside which the Council
would invite you to participate in. Thank

01:02:52.980 --> 01:02:53.230
you.

